  "id": "Compound::DB01236 - Gene::2565",
  "verbalization": "Sevoflurane, a commonly used anesthetic agent, is shown to bind to Gabrg3, Gabra6, and Gabrp, all of which are subunits of the Gaba-a receptor. These receptors are critical for the modulation of neuronal activity, and their participation in Gaba-a receptor activity is a well-documented biological process. The integration of these binding interactions suggests that Sevoflurane may influence the function of Gaba-a receptors, potentially altering neurotransmitter signaling. This mechanism could contribute to the anesthetic effects of Sevoflurane by modulating synaptic transmission. The shared involvement of these receptors in Gaba-a receptor activity implies a common pathway through which Sevoflurane may exert its effects on neuronal function. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Sevoflurane and Gabrg1, as all three receptors are part of the same functional pathway, and their participation in Gaba-a receptor activity is a consistent feature across the visible paths.",
